-- title:
--   Descent of approximate invariants along powers of t
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $G$ a finite group acting on $R$ by ring automorphisms, and let $t \in R$ satisfy $g \cdot t = t$ for every $g \in G$ and be a non-zero-divisor in the form: $t x = 0$ implies $x = 0$. Fix $e \in \mathbb{N}$ and assume the following explicit bound on $t$-power torsion in the first cohomology: for every map $y : G \to R$ satisfying the cocycle identity $y(gh) = g \cdot y(h) + y(g)$ for all $g, h \in G$, if there exist $k \in \mathbb{N}$ and $z \in R$ with $t^{k} y(g) = g \cdot z - z$ for all $g$, then there exists $z \in R$ with $t^{e} y(g) = g \cdot z - z$ for all $g$. Let $m \in \mathbb{N}$ with $e \le m$, and let $x \in R$ be such that $g \cdot x - x$ lies in the ideal generated by $t^{m}$ for every $g \in G$. The conclusion is that there exists $w \in R$ with $g \cdot w = w$ for all $g \in G$ and $x - w$ in the ideal generated by $t^{m-e}$ (truncated subtraction, here equal to the ordinary difference).
--
--   This is the elementary "bounded shift" comparison between elements of $R$ that are $G$-invariant modulo $t^{m}$ and genuine invariants: at the cost of $e$ levels in the $t$-adic filtration, an approximate invariant may be replaced by an exact one. It is used in the study of invariants of admissible algebras under flat base change, being cited by [`AlgebraicGeometry.AdmissibleAlgebra.exists_forall_sub_tmul_mem_span_pow_of_flat`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.AdmissibleAlgebra.exists_forall_sub_tmul_mem_span_pow_of_flat).
